I2C1 CONTROL - SCAN AND TRANSACTIONS

Device /dev/i2c/1 uses GP26 SDA, GP27 SCL. The i2cctl command
uses a 7-bit address between 0x08 and 0x77, transfer <=32 B.

DISCOVERY AND VALIDATION
  pins
  i2cctl scan              read-based address probing
  help i2cctl

EXACT COMMANDS (userland/apps/i2cctl.c)
  i2cctl read ADDR COUNT
  i2cctl write ADDR BYTE...
  i2cctl xfer ADDR READ_COUNT [BYTE...]
  i2cctl scan
  i2cctl read 0x23 2
  i2cctl xfer 0x23 2 0x10
The last two are only examples; they require a compatible device.
Bytes may be decimal or 0x-prefixed hex. COUNT in 1..32.
A scan sends a one-byte read to each candidate; it is not a
zero-length/side-effect-free address probe for all devices.

BEFORE A WRITE
  1) verify sensor identity, supply and 7-bit address;
  2) read its data sheet for command/register meanings;
  3) identify whether the target supports raw read vs combined
     write+read, and any required delay;
  4) obtain consent before setting registers;
  5) perform a bounded read to verify.

SENSOR COMMAND
  bh1750 [count]          example light sensor utility
  0 count may mean continuous running: avoid through MCP.

PYTHON API
  i2c_write(fd, addr, list)
  i2c_read(fd, addr, count)
  i2c_xfer(fd, addr, list, count)
  Python APIs require an open device descriptor; consult
  /man/languages/python for the limited assignment syntax.
